ay VF and FH = |FV − VH|, giving FH² = FV² + VH² − 2·FV·VH. Hence VP_x² = VF·VH. (Vector form: 0 = vec(FP_x)·vec(P_xP_y) = (vec(FV) + vec(VP_x))·(vec(VH) + vec(VP_x)) = vec(FV)·vec(VH) + VP_x².) (5) Therefore every position of P_y satisfies HP_y² = 4·VP_x² = 4·VF·VH (equivalently HP_y² = 2·FD·VH = 4·VE·VJ). Take the frame with origin V, x-axis the ray VF along j, and y-axis h1. Then P_y = (x, y) with x = VH and |y| = HP_y, and the equation reads y² = 4·VF·x. This is a parabola with vertex V, axis j and vertex tangent h1. Conversely, P_x = (0, s) gives P_y = (s²/VF, 2s), so P_y sweeps the whole parabola. Its focus is F and its directrix is e; this is consistent with, but not used in, the derivation. Combining (A) and (B): EJ = KP_y = VF + VP_x²/VF, i.e. EJ·VF = KP_y·VF = FP_x². Data check: VE = VF = VD ≈ 2.309, VJ = VH ≈ 9.036, EJ = KP_y ≈ 11.345, HP_y² ≈ 83.47 ≈ 4·VF·VH.", "closes_goal": true}
